window.QUIZ2_BANK.push(...[{"id":601,"targetId":601,"type":"tc","text":"The director expected frantic activity before the premiere; instead, rehearsal proceeded at a {{1}} pace, with long pauses between even the simplest cues.","choices":[["languid","frenetic","punctilious","precipitous","contentious"]],"answers":[["languid"]],"explanation":"Long pauses contrast with expected urgency, requiring languid, or unhurried; haste, precision, and conflict do not explain the pauses.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":602,"targetId":602,"type":"tc","text":"Although the archive received a prestigious grant, its collections continued to {{1}} in unopened boxes because none of the money could be spent on staff.","choices":[["languish","proliferate","coalesce","flourish","resonate"]],"answers":[["languish"]],"explanation":"The collections remain neglected and inactive: they languish. Funding without processing does not establish growth or successful integration.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":603,"targetId":603,"type":"tc","text":"The donor called the endowment an investment, but it offered no return and no public recognition; it was more accurately an act of {{1}}.","choices":[["largesse","avarice","litigation","chicanery","retribution"]],"answers":[["largesse"]],"explanation":"Giving resources without compensation exemplifies largesse. Greed, legal conflict, deception, and revenge are unsupported.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":604,"targetId":604,"type":"tc","text":"The rescue plan proved impractical, yet its aim of protecting residents whom every previous plan had ignored was undeniably {{1}}.","choices":[["laudable","nefarious","derivative","equivocal","negligible"]],"answers":[["laudable"]],"explanation":"The concession distinguishes a failed method from a laudable, praiseworthy purpose. Neither evil intent nor insignificance fits the protective aim.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":605,"targetId":605,"type":"tc","text":"The foundation’s {{1}} reception contrasted with the {{2}} of its research funding: the dinner cost more than all the year’s fellowships.","choices":[["lavish","austere","furtive"],["paucity","abundance","efficacy"]],"answers":[["lavish"],["paucity"]],"explanation":"An extravagant reception is lavish; the very small research allocation establishes paucity. Efficacy concerns effectiveness rather than quantity.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"paucity","meaning":"An insufficient quantity"}],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":606,"targetId":606,"type":"tc","text":"Inspection had become {{1}}, allowing obvious safety breaches to pass unpunished; the report blamed official {{2}}, rather than a shortage of inspectors.","choices":[["lax","stringent","infallible"],["imprudence","deftness","prescience"]],"answers":[["lax"],["imprudence"]],"explanation":"Lax enforcement fails to punish breaches. The report faults imprudent judgment rather than staffing, skill, or foresight.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"imprudence","meaning":"Failure to exercise sensible caution"}],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":607,"targetId":607,"type":"tc","text":"Far from invigorating the committee, endless disputes left its members {{1}}; even a brief discussion now demanded an {{2}} effort.","choices":[["lethargic","ebullient","impetuous"],["onerous","effortless","illusory"]],"answers":[["lethargic"],["onerous"]],"explanation":"Exhaustion makes lethargic appropriate; a burdensome, onerous effort reinforces their depleted energy rather than suggesting ease.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":608,"targetId":608,"type":"tc","text":"The minister’s former triumph became a {{1}} once its hidden costs emerged; the resulting {{2}} brought attention, but none of the {{3}} he had enjoyed when he was widely admired.","choices":[["liability","windfall","panacea"],["notoriety","anonymity","equanimity"],["prestige","obloquy","hostility"]],"answers":[["liability"],["notoriety"],["prestige"]],"explanation":"The triumph becomes a disadvantage, or liability. Discreditable attention is notoriety, which contrasts with the prestige of formerly being widely admired.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"notoriety","meaning":"Fame acquired through discreditable conduct"},{"word":"prestige","meaning":"Respect and admiration attached to high standing"}],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":609,"targetId":609,"type":"se","text":"His memoir celebrates the life of a {{1}}, presenting abandoned moral restraints and excessive sensual indulgence as freedoms rather than failings.","choices":[["libertine","debauchee","ascetic","recluse","moralist","philanthropist"]],"answers":[["libertine","debauchee"]],"explanation":"Libertine and debauchee both describe indulgence without conventional moral restraint; the alternatives do not identify that lifestyle.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"debauchee","meaning":"Person devoted to excessive sensual pleasures"}],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":610,"targetId":610,"type":"se","text":"After the silt settled, the water was so {{1}} that the smallest stones on the streambed could be counted from the bank.","choices":[["limpid","transparent","turbid","viscous","brackish","tepid"]],"answers":[["limpid","transparent"]],"explanation":"Limpid and transparent both explain unobstructed vision through the water; temperature, saltiness, and thickness do not.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"transparent","meaning":"Allowing light to pass through clearly"}],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":611,"targetId":611,"type":"tc","text":"The newspaper rushed to {{1}} the entrepreneur, treating one profitable venture as proof of almost supernatural business judgment.","choices":[["lionize","ostracize","interrogate","emulate","restrain"]],"answers":[["lionize"]],"explanation":"Exaggerated celebration is to lionize. The paper neither rejects nor interrogates the entrepreneur.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":612,"targetId":612,"type":"tc","text":"The critic could admire the architect’s ingenuity and still {{1}} the buildings’ effect on the neighborhood, which he called a permanent blight.","choices":[["loathe","endorse","replicate","discount","commend"]],"answers":[["loathe"]],"explanation":"Calling the effect a blight supports intense dislike, or loathe, despite admiration for technique.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":613,"targetId":613,"type":"tc","text":"Normally {{1}}, the witness answered in monosyllables, a change that made her friends suspect that she had been warned to say little.","choices":[["loquacious","reticent","impartial","methodical","phlegmatic"]],"answers":[["loquacious"]],"explanation":"The striking contrast with short answers requires her usual talkativeness, or loquacity; habitual reserve would not explain the surprise.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":614,"targetId":614,"type":"tc","text":"The theorem is difficult, but its exposition is remarkably {{1}}: every unfamiliar symbol is explained before it appears in an argument.","choices":[["lucid","elliptical","fallacious","tendentious","impenetrable"]],"answers":[["lucid"]],"explanation":"Careful explanations make the exposition lucid despite the subject’s difficulty. The other choices imply obscurity, invalidity, or bias.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":615,"targetId":615,"type":"tc","text":"The service was {{1}} for its owners, but critics questioned its social {{2}}: it produced substantial profit without measurably improving clients’ outcomes.","choices":[["lucrative","ruinous","gratuitous"],["efficacy","notoriety","opulence"]],"answers":[["lucrative"],["efficacy"]],"explanation":"Profit makes the service lucrative; unchanged outcomes raise doubts about its effectiveness, or efficacy, rather than fame or wealth.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"efficacy","meaning":"Ability to produce an intended result"}],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":616,"targetId":616,"type":"tc","text":"The speaker’s {{1}} delivery seemed disproportionate to the minor setback; listeners wanted a practical explanation, not an {{2}} for a project still alive.","choices":[["lugubrious","buoyant","nonchalant"],["elegy","incentive","innovation"]],"answers":[["lugubrious"],["elegy"]],"explanation":"Exaggerated mourning is lugubrious. An elegy laments loss and is misplaced for a continuing project.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"elegy","meaning":"Poem or composition mourning a death or loss"}],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":617,"targetId":617,"type":"tc","text":"The brief {{1}} in negotiations did not signal agreement; it gave the mediator time to {{2}} the disputed clauses before argument resumed.","choices":[["lull","crescendo","culmination"],["explicate","obscure","retract"]],"answers":[["lull"],["explicate"]],"explanation":"A quiet interval is a lull. Explicate means explain fully, matching the mediator’s clarifying work rather than concealment or withdrawal.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"explicate","meaning":"Explain something carefully and fully"}],"section":13,"initial":"l"},{"id":618,"targetId":618,"type":"tc","text":"Marketed as family entertainment, the exhibition featured {{1}} scenes of decay and violent death; its publicity was thus {{2}}, and parents’ complaints were hardly {{3}}.","choices":[["macabre","pastoral","jocular"],["misleading","prescient","comprehensive"],["unwarranted","predictable","vehement"]],"answers":[["macabre"],["misleading"],["unwarranted"]],"explanation":"Gruesome death scenes are macabre and contradict the family-oriented publicity, making it misleading. Complaints were justified, hence hardly unwarranted.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"misleading","meaning":"Likely to lead someone to a false conclusion"},{"word":"unwarranted","meaning":"Not supported by sufficient reason"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":619,"targetId":619,"type":"se","text":"Her {{1}} account of the excavation combined command of the evidence with a confidence that made veteran archaeologists inclined to accept her conclusions.","choices":[["magisterial","authoritative","tentative","diffident","fragmentary","frivolous"]],"answers":[["magisterial","authoritative"]],"explanation":"Magisterial and authoritative both convey commanding expertise; uncertainty, incompleteness, and triviality undermine such authority.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"authoritative","meaning":"Carrying confident expertise or recognized authority"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":620,"targetId":620,"type":"se","text":"Although empowered to dismiss his defeated rival, the leader was {{1}}, offering her a substantial role without demanding an apology.","choices":[["magnanimous","forgiving","vindictive","parsimonious","irascible","suspicious"]],"answers":[["magnanimous","forgiving"]],"explanation":"Magnanimous and forgiving fit generosity toward a rival whom he could punish; revenge and resentment are contradicted.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"forgiving","meaning":"Willing to release resentment for an offense"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":621,"targetId":621,"type":"tc","text":"After the replication failed, the author continued to {{1}} that her original result was genuine, though she could not explain the discrepancy.","choices":[["maintain","concede","retract","improvise","recant"]],"answers":[["maintain"]],"explanation":"Maintaining a proposition means continuing to assert it. Retracting or recanting would reverse her position.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":622,"targetId":622,"type":"tc","text":"The diplomat’s conciliatory gesture was {{1}}: praising the host’s unexpectedly modern manners introduced an insult that had not previously been at issue.","choices":[["maladroit","adroit","judicious","circumspect","tactful"]],"answers":[["maladroit"]],"explanation":"Creating an insult while attempting conciliation demonstrates maladroit, clumsy handling rather than tact or skill.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":623,"targetId":623,"type":"tc","text":"The audit separated clerical mistakes from {{1}}, reserving the latter term for officials who deliberately transferred public funds to personal accounts.","choices":[["malfeasance","ineptitude","procrastination","heterodoxy","diffidence"]],"answers":[["malfeasance"]],"explanation":"Deliberate wrongdoing in public office is malfeasance. Incompetence and delay lack the specified intentional abuse.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":624,"targetId":624,"type":"tc","text":"Unable to refute the results, the columnist tried to {{1}} the scientist’s character, circulating damaging allegations he had never verified.","choices":[["malign","vindicate","emulate","appease","exonerate"]],"answers":[["malign"]],"explanation":"Unverified disparagement maligns someone. Defending, copying, and calming the scientist do not describe the attack.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":625,"targetId":625,"type":"tc","text":"The committee proved {{1}}: a single forceful speaker could reverse its recommendation, suggesting that consensus reflected influence more than independent {{2}}.","choices":[["malleable","obdurate","immutable"],["judgment","compliance","deference"]],"answers":[["malleable"],["judgment"]],"explanation":"Being easily reshaped makes the committee malleable. Independent judgment contrasts with influence; compliance and deference repeat that influence.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"judgment","meaning":"Ability to form a considered evaluation"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":626,"targetId":626,"type":"tc","text":"The proposed rules would {{1}} researchers to one approved procedure, critics argued, replacing intellectual freedom with {{2}} obedience.","choices":[["manacle","liberate","elevate"],["unquestioning","skeptical","innovative"]],"answers":[["manacle"],["unquestioning"]],"explanation":"Manacle means restrain, here figuratively. A compulsory method demands unquestioning obedience, whereas skepticism and innovation preserve freedom.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"unquestioning","meaning":"Accepting without challenging or examining"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":627,"targetId":627,"type":"tc","text":"The defect became {{1}} only under magnification; earlier assurances of perfection were therefore {{2}}, having rested on inspection by unaided eyes.","choices":[["manifest","imperceptible","latent"],["erroneous","incontrovertible","prescient"]],"answers":[["manifest"],["erroneous"]],"explanation":"Magnification makes the defect manifest, or evident. A real defect makes assurances of perfection erroneous, not conclusive or prescient.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"erroneous","meaning":"Incorrect or mistaken"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":628,"targetId":628,"type":"tc","text":"Scheduling consultations during working hours would {{1}} hourly employees; the council’s claim of {{2}} participation was thus {{3}} by the process itself.","choices":[["marginalize","empower","compensate"],["comprehensive","selective","nominal"],["belied","vindicated","amplified"]],"answers":[["marginalize"],["comprehensive"],["belied"]],"explanation":"Exclusion marginalizes employees. A claim of comprehensive participation is contradicted, or belied, by that exclusion.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"belied","meaning":"Contradicted by evidence or appearance"},{"word":"comprehensive","meaning":"Covering all important aspects"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":629,"targetId":629,"type":"se","text":"Where advancement usually rewarded conformity, she remained a {{1}}, rejecting the prevailing theory even when dissent cost her influential supporters.","choices":[["maverick","nonconformist","sycophant","traditionalist","mediator","neophyte"]],"answers":[["maverick","nonconformist"]],"explanation":"Maverick and nonconformist convey independence from accepted views. The other roles do not require dissent.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"nonconformist","meaning":"Person who refuses to follow prevailing conventions"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":630,"targetId":630,"type":"se","text":"The finale was {{1}}, layering tearful reunions and sentimental music so insistently that previously believable grief began to seem manufactured.","choices":[["mawkish","saccharine","austere","dispassionate","mordant","laconic"]],"answers":[["mawkish","saccharine"]],"explanation":"Mawkish and saccharine both criticize excessive sentimentality; restraint or sarcasm would contradict the emotional excess.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"saccharine","meaning":"Excessively sweet or sentimental"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":631,"targetId":631,"type":"tc","text":"Faulty memory might explain the memoir’s errors, but the author admitted inventing several incidents deliberately; this admission established {{1}}.","choices":[["mendacity","naivete","equanimity","nostalgia","imprudence"]],"answers":[["mendacity"]],"explanation":"Deliberate invention establishes mendacity, or dishonesty, rather than an innocent lapse of memory or mere poor judgment.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":632,"targetId":632,"type":"tc","text":"Her readiness to defend either side whenever its fee was higher gave the consultant’s professed ideological commitment a {{1}} cast.","choices":[["mercenary","altruistic","ascetic","impartial","disinterested"]],"answers":[["mercenary"]],"explanation":"Money determines allegiance, making mercenary appropriate. Impartiality requires neutrality rather than sale to the highest bidder.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":633,"targetId":633,"type":"tc","text":"The supervisor’s {{1}} temperament complicated planning: a proposal praised at breakfast could be condemned by noon without any change in its contents.","choices":[["mercurial","equable","phlegmatic","resolute","steadfast"]],"answers":[["mercurial"]],"explanation":"Unpredictable mood changes are mercurial; each alternative describes some form of steadiness.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":634,"targetId":634,"type":"tc","text":"The panel considered the project {{1}} despite its failed prediction, rewarding rigorous methods and the valuable negative findings.","choices":[["meritorious","perfunctory","duplicitous","negligible","derivative"]],"answers":[["meritorious"]],"explanation":"The panel finds it deserving of reward, or meritorious. Failure of a prediction does not erase valuable rigorous work.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":635,"targetId":635,"type":"tc","text":"Calling the city a living archive is {{1}}, not literal; the essay’s subsequent {{2}} explains how buildings preserve collective experience without themselves remembering.","choices":[["metaphorical","anatomical","empirical"],["exposition","digression","retraction"]],"answers":[["metaphorical"],["exposition"]],"explanation":"The implied comparison is metaphorical. A systematic explanation is an exposition, not a withdrawal or unrelated digression.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"exposition","meaning":"A systematic explanation of a subject"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":636,"targetId":636,"type":"tc","text":"The editor was {{1}} about sources, checking every quotation; her reputation for {{2}} came from this sustained care rather than spectacular discoveries.","choices":[["meticulous","cavalier","desultory"],["scrupulousness","audacity","improvisation"]],"answers":[["meticulous"],["scrupulousness"]],"explanation":"Checking every detail makes her meticulous. Scrupulousness names that attention to accuracy; boldness and improvisation do not.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"scrupulousness","meaning":"Careful attention to accuracy and proper conduct"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":637,"targetId":637,"type":"tc","text":"The recruits were {{1}}, eager to attempt the dangerous crossing, but their leader feared that courage without experience would become {{2}}.","choices":[["mettlesome","timorous","apathetic"],["imprudence","deftness","equanimity"]],"answers":[["mettlesome"],["imprudence"]],"explanation":"Spirited courage makes them mettlesome; without sensible caution it risks becoming imprudence, rather than skill or calm.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"imprudence","meaning":"Failure to exercise sensible caution"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":638,"targetId":638,"type":"tc","text":"The software could {{1}} a pianist’s phrasing without understanding it; its impressive {{2}} should not be confused with the {{3}} of an original interpretation.","choices":[["mimic","evaluate","repudiate"],["imitation","disregard","reticence"],["creativity","predictability","uniformity"]],"answers":[["mimic"],["imitation"],["creativity"]],"explanation":"Copying is to mimic and produces imitation. Original interpretation requires creativity, which mere replication does not demonstrate.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"creativity","meaning":"Ability to produce original ideas or interpretations"},{"word":"imitation","meaning":"An attempt to reproduce another’s behavior or work"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":639,"targetId":639,"type":"se","text":"The discrepancy was so {{1}} that rounding the measurements to the nearest hundredth made it disappear.","choices":[["minute","minuscule","prodigious","conspicuous","pervasive","consequential"]],"answers":[["minute","minuscule"]],"explanation":"Minute and minuscule mean extremely small. The other choices do not explain why ordinary rounding erases the discrepancy.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"minuscule","meaning":"Extremely small"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":640,"targetId":640,"type":"tc","text":"His repeated claim that all human company was intolerable made the novelist seem a {{1}}, although affectionate letters to a few friends complicated that reputation.","choices":[["misanthrope","humanitarian","socialite","sycophant","extrovert"]],"answers":[["misanthrope"]],"explanation":"A professed dislike of all human company identifies a misanthrope. Affection for some individuals complicates the reputation but does not turn it into sociability.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":641,"targetId":641,"type":"tc","text":"The essay distrusts institutions but is not {{1}}: it repeatedly emphasizes ordinary people’s capacity for kindness and cooperation.","choices":[["misanthropic","analytical","tentative","unorthodox","polemical"]],"answers":[["misanthropic"]],"explanation":"Belief in human kindness rules out a misanthropic outlook. Criticism of institutions can still be analytical or unconventional.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":642,"targetId":642,"type":"tc","text":"Readers who interpret the narrator’s silence as approval {{1}} the scene; earlier chapters establish that she withdraws when most deeply opposed.","choices":[["misconstrue","explicate","corroborate","embellish","vindicate"]],"answers":[["misconstrue"]],"explanation":"Approval contradicts the established significance of her silence, so those readers misconstrue the scene rather than explain it correctly.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":643,"targetId":643,"type":"tc","text":"The comedy requires a {{1}} whom viewers can condemn without qualification, yet its supposed villain behaves more decently than its heroes.","choices":[["miscreant","benefactor","arbiter","prodigy","sage"]],"answers":[["miscreant"]],"explanation":"An unequivocally wicked person is a miscreant. The humor arises because the supposed villain does not fit that role.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":644,"targetId":644,"type":"se","text":"Able to finance the whole library, the patron refused even to replace a broken lamp, a refusal the staff judged {{1}} rather than prudent.","choices":[["miserly","stingy","munificent","profligate","improvident","effusive"]],"answers":[["miserly","stingy"]],"explanation":"Miserly and stingy both describe extreme unwillingness to spend despite ample means. Generosity and reckless spending contradict the refusal.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"stingy","meaning":"Unwilling to spend or share resources"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":645,"targetId":645,"type":"tc","text":"Calling the panel independent was a {{1}}: every member could be dismissed by the mayor, and its findings were consistently {{2}} in his favor.","choices":[["misnomer","truism","eulogy"],["biased","impartial","ambivalent"]],"answers":[["misnomer"],["biased"]],"explanation":"Independent is a misleading name, or misnomer. Findings favoring the controlling mayor are biased rather than impartial or mixed.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"biased","meaning":"Unfairly favoring a particular side"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":646,"targetId":646,"type":"tc","text":"The buffer could {{1}} flood damage but not eliminate it; officials damaged their {{2}} by guaranteeing protection the engineering could not deliver.","choices":[["mitigate","exacerbate","perpetuate"],["credibility","obscurity","reticence"]],"answers":[["mitigate"],["credibility"]],"explanation":"The buffer reduces severity, hence mitigate. Unsupported guarantees undermine credibility, not obscurity or silence.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"credibility","meaning":"The quality of deserving belief or trust"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":647,"targetId":647,"type":"tc","text":"Her ambitions were {{1}}: a reliable account of one neighborhood, not the {{2}} survey of all urban life that reviewers inexplicably demanded.","choices":[["modest","grandiose","boundless"],["comprehensive","fragmentary","parochial"]],"answers":[["modest"],["comprehensive"]],"explanation":"Her deliberately limited goal is modest. An all-encompassing survey is comprehensive and supplies the contrast.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"comprehensive","meaning":"Covering all important aspects"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":648,"targetId":648,"type":"tc","text":"The gallery rejected {{1}} paintings that merely echoed current fashion, preferring work whose interest would {{2}}; it valued {{3}} appeal over immediate popularity.","choices":[["modish","archaic","unclassifiable"],["endure","dissipate","plummet"],["lasting","ephemeral","transient"]],"answers":[["modish"],["endure"],["lasting"]],"explanation":"Fashion-following paintings are modish. Work that survives changing tastes endures and has lasting appeal.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"endure","meaning":"Continue to exist through time or difficulty"},{"word":"lasting","meaning":"Continuing for a long time"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":649,"targetId":649,"type":"se","text":"The apology sought to {{1}} the angry delegates by acknowledging the insult and promising that it would not be repeated.","choices":[["mollify","appease","antagonize","embolden","deceive","disperse"]],"answers":[["mollify","appease"]],"explanation":"Mollify and appease both mean calm the anger through conciliatory action, which the acknowledgment and promise provide.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":650,"targetId":650,"type":"se","text":"The improvement was {{1}}: before the researchers could arrange a second measurement, the system had reverted to its previous state.","choices":[["momentary","fleeting","durable","incremental","inexorable","cumulative"]],"answers":[["momentary","fleeting"]],"explanation":"Momentary and fleeting mean short-lived. Rapid reversal contradicts duration and does not imply gradual accumulation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"fleeting","meaning":"Lasting only a very short time"}],"section":13,"initial":"m"},{"id":651,"targetId":651,"type":"tc","text":"The film’s location changes constantly, yet its dramatic pattern is {{1}}: every encounter reproduces the same quarrel and ends with the same reconciliation.","choices":[["monotonous","munificent","protean","unprecedented","eclectic"]],"answers":[["monotonous"]],"explanation":"Repeated identical dramatic structures make the pattern monotonous, despite changing settings. Variety and novelty do not fit.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"m"},{"id":652,"targetId":652,"type":"tc","text":"The reviewer’s humor was {{1}}, as when she described a supposedly original argument as a triumph of amnesia over bibliography.","choices":[["mordant","reverential","munificent","mawkish","guileless"]],"answers":[["mordant"]],"explanation":"The barbed remark mocks the author’s originality, making mordant, or bitingly sarcastic, apt; sentiment and reverence would not produce the insult.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"m"},{"id":653,"targetId":653,"type":"tc","text":"Despite a series of small successes, the team remained {{1}}, interpreting each improvement as merely a prelude to another inevitable disappointment.","choices":[["morose","jubilant","munificent","sanguine","nonchalant"]],"answers":[["morose"]],"explanation":"The gloomy interpretation of success shows moroseness rather than optimism or casual unconcern.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"m"},{"id":654,"targetId":654,"type":"tc","text":"The correspondence is valuable precisely because it is {{1}}: accounts of meals, errands, and household repairs reveal daily life that official histories ignore.","choices":[["mundane","munificent","numinous","portentous","recondite"]],"answers":[["mundane"]],"explanation":"Ordinary domestic activities make the letters mundane. Their historical value does not make their content mystical, ominous, or esoteric.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"m"},{"id":655,"targetId":655,"type":"tc","text":"The grant was {{1}}, covering not only tuition but every living expense; recipients were astonished by the donor’s {{2}}.","choices":[["munificent","paltry","nominal"],["magnanimity","avarice","reticence"]],"answers":[["munificent"],["magnanimity"]],"explanation":"Extensive support is munificent, and recipients admire generous spirit, or magnanimity. Stinginess or silence would not account for the award.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"magnanimity","meaning":"Generosity toward someone one could punish or resent"}],"section":14,"initial":"m"},{"id":656,"targetId":656,"type":"tc","text":"The captain mistook the sailors’ complaints for harmless grumbling until their coordinated refusal to obey revealed a {{1}}; his earlier confidence was plainly {{2}}.","choices":[["mutiny","eulogy","respite"],["erroneous","prescient","vindicated"]],"answers":[["mutiny"],["erroneous"]],"explanation":"Collective rebellion against authority is mutiny. Its occurrence makes the captain’s reassuring interpretation erroneous.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"erroneous","meaning":"Incorrect or mistaken"}],"section":14,"initial":"m"},{"id":657,"targetId":657,"type":"tc","text":"The policy was {{1}}, boosting this year’s revenue by exhausting resources that future budgets would require; immediate gain displaced consideration of {{2}} effects.","choices":[["myopic","prescient","providential"],["consequential","illusory","negligible"]],"answers":[["myopic"],["consequential"]],"explanation":"Ignoring future resource loss is myopic. Those future effects matter, hence consequential, rather than negligible or imaginary.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"consequential","meaning":"Having important effects"}],"section":14,"initial":"m"},{"id":658,"targetId":658,"type":"tc","text":"The survey gathered {{1}} observations, yet quantity did not ensure {{2}}: because every respondent came from one neighborhood, a national inference remained {{3}}.","choices":[["myriad","scant","nominal"],["representativeness","monotony","redundancy"],["unwarranted","incontrovertible","inescapable"]],"answers":[["myriad"],["representativeness"],["unwarranted"]],"explanation":"Myriad means very numerous. A large localized sample need not represent the nation, so the national inference is unwarranted despite its size.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"representativeness","meaning":"The extent to which a sample reflects a larger population"},{"word":"unwarranted","meaning":"Not supported by sufficient reason"}],"section":14,"initial":"m"},{"id":659,"targetId":659,"type":"se","text":"The bankruptcy marked the {{1}} of the company’s fortunes, a point from which even its modest subsequent recovery represented an improvement.","choices":[["nadir","low point","zenith","apogee","turning point","plateau"]],"answers":[["nadir","low point"]],"explanation":"Nadir and low point both identify the worst position. A turning point need not be the lowest, while zenith and apogee are highest points.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"low point","meaning":"The worst or lowest stage"}],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":660,"targetId":660,"type":"se","text":"Trusting every promise from the rival bidder, the {{1}} negotiator disclosed the firm’s reservation price before asking for any concession.","choices":[["naive","gullible","canny","cynical","circumspect","duplicitous"]],"answers":[["naive","gullible"]],"explanation":"Naive and gullible both fit uncritical trust. The action contradicts caution or suspicion, and nothing indicates that the negotiator is deceiving others.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":661,"targetId":661,"type":"tc","text":"The theory is still {{1}}: its central concepts are being defined, and no agreed method yet exists for testing its claims.","choices":[["nascent","entrenched","munificent","obsolete","immutable"]],"answers":[["nascent"]],"explanation":"A theory whose basic concepts are still forming is nascent. Established, obsolete, and unchangeable would misdescribe this early stage.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":662,"targetId":662,"type":"tc","text":"The archivist was not merely unlucky but {{1}}: she had repeatedly ignored warnings that the storage room’s leaking roof endangered the papers.","choices":[["negligent","munificent","meticulous","prescient","punctilious"]],"answers":[["negligent"]],"explanation":"Ignoring repeated warnings is a failure of reasonable care, or negligence, rather than bad luck or careful conduct.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":663,"targetId":663,"type":"tc","text":"Although statistically detectable, the difference was practically {{1}}: users could neither perceive it nor gain any measurable benefit from it.","choices":[["negligible","salient","munificent","momentous","profound"]],"answers":[["negligible"]],"explanation":"A detectable difference can still be too small to matter, hence negligible. The other choices suggest importance.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":664,"targetId":664,"type":"tc","text":"Only a {{1}} would mistake the laboratory’s tidy demonstration for normal research, the veteran remarked; experienced workers knew how many failed trials it concealed.","choices":[["neophyte","munificent","polymath","virtuoso","luminary"]],"answers":[["neophyte"]],"explanation":"The contrast with experienced workers identifies a beginner, or neophyte, rather than an accomplished expert.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":665,"targetId":665,"type":"tc","text":"The problem was {{1}} rather than catastrophic: it repeatedly interrupted work, but the interruptions did not threaten the project’s {{2}}.","choices":[["nettlesome","auspicious","trivial"],["viability","monotony","obscurity"]],"answers":[["nettlesome"],["viability"]],"explanation":"Repeated irritating interruptions are nettlesome. Their failure to threaten continued success preserves viability; trivial understates repeated disruption.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"viability","meaning":"Capacity to function or succeed over time"}],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":666,"targetId":666,"type":"tc","text":"The second reagent was introduced to {{1}} the first one’s acidity; the chemist expected the two effects to {{2}} each other rather than accumulate.","choices":[["neutralize","intensify","propagate"],["offset","enhance","corroborate"]],"answers":[["neutralize"],["offset"]],"explanation":"To counteract acidity is to neutralize it. Offset likewise means counterbalance, which contrasts with accumulation or enhancement.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"offset","meaning":"Counterbalance or counteract an effect"}],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":667,"targetId":667,"type":"tc","text":"Her {{1}} response to the unexpected objection impressed colleagues more than the prepared speech; it revealed a {{2}} that rehearsal alone could not explain.","choices":[["nimble","plodding","ponderous"],["deftness","torpor","dogma"]],"answers":[["nimble"],["deftness"]],"explanation":"Quick, agile thinking is nimble and shows deftness, or skill. Sluggishness and rigid doctrine do not explain an effective spontaneous response.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"deftness","meaning":"Skill in performing something neatly and effectively"}],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":668,"targetId":668,"type":"tc","text":"The appeal invoked {{1}} ideals of public service, but the payments exposed official {{2}}; declarations of virtue had served to {{3}} private enrichment.","choices":[["noble","ignoble","mercenary"],["venality","probity","temperance"],["conceal","repudiate","impede"]],"answers":[["noble"],["venality"],["conceal"]],"explanation":"Honorable ideals are noble. Corrupt payments demonstrate venality, and the contrast shows that virtuous claims hid, or concealed, enrichment rather than blocked it.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"conceal","meaning":"Hide from observation or knowledge"},{"word":"venality","meaning":"Readiness to misuse a position in exchange for money"}],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":669,"targetId":669,"type":"se","text":"The membership fee was {{1}}, set at one dollar so that cost would exclude almost nobody while preserving a formal registration requirement.","choices":[["nominal","minimal","onerous","exorbitant","prohibitive","remunerative"]],"answers":[["nominal","minimal"]],"explanation":"Nominal and minimal both convey a very small charge. The aim to exclude almost nobody rules out costly fees.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"minimal","meaning":"Very small in amount or degree"}],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":670,"targetId":670,"type":"se","text":"While the rest of the team anxiously monitored the vote, she seemed {{1}}, reading a novel and scarcely looking up when the results were announced.","choices":[["nonchalant","unconcerned","agitated","apprehensive","indignant","exuberant"]],"answers":[["nonchalant","unconcerned"]],"explanation":"Nonchalant and unconcerned describe the displayed lack of anxiety. Fear, anger, and excitement conflict with her casual behavior.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"unconcerned","meaning":"Showing little anxiety or interest"}],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":671,"targetId":671,"type":"tc","text":"Asked why the machine worked only when unplugged, the engineer was {{1}}; the observation contradicted every mechanism she had considered.","choices":[["nonplussed","complacent","munificent","prescient","dogmatic"]],"answers":[["nonplussed"]],"explanation":"A baffling contradiction leaves her bewildered and unable to respond, or nonplussed. Confidence and foreknowledge do not fit.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":672,"targetId":672,"type":"tc","text":"The fragrance initially made the vapor seem harmless, but its {{1}} effects became evident when nearby plants withered after repeated exposure.","choices":[["noxious","salubrious","munificent","ineffable","transient"]],"answers":[["noxious"]],"explanation":"Withering after exposure indicates harm, so noxious fits despite pleasant smell. Salubrious would suggest benefit.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":673,"targetId":673,"type":"tc","text":"The translation preserves the argument’s outline but loses a crucial {{1}}: the original distinguishes reluctant acceptance from enthusiastic approval.","choices":[["nuance","munificent","truism","digression","anomaly"]],"answers":[["nuance"]],"explanation":"The subtle difference between kinds of assent is a nuance. It is not an unrelated excursion or an abnormal event.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":674,"targetId":674,"type":"tc","text":"Because the agreement was signed under coercion, the court could {{1}} it, releasing both parties from obligations that would otherwise remain binding.","choices":[["nullify","ratify","munificent","codify","perpetuate"]],"answers":[["nullify"]],"explanation":"Making the agreement legally void is to nullify it. Ratifying or perpetuating it would preserve the obligations.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":675,"targetId":675,"type":"tc","text":"The chamber’s {{1}} atmosphere inspired spiritual awe even in secular visitors; its power lay in suggestion, not in any explicit claim about the {{2}}.","choices":[["numinous","prosaic","clinical"],["occult","mundane","quotidian"]],"answers":[["numinous"],["occult"]],"explanation":"Numinous describes an atmosphere evoking spiritual awe. Occult refers to supernatural matters; mundane and quotidian concern ordinary life rather than the implied spiritual realm.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"occult","meaning":"Supernatural or mystical matters"}],"section":14,"initial":"n"},{"id":676,"targetId":676,"type":"tc","text":"Even after each objection was answered, the director remained {{1}}; the committee learned that his resistance arose from {{2}}, not uncertainty about the evidence.","choices":[["obdurate","amenable","tractable"],["intransigence","diffidence","curiosity"]],"answers":[["obdurate"],["intransigence"]],"explanation":"Resistance unchanged by evidence makes the director obdurate. Intransigence identifies an unwillingness to compromise, whereas diffidence or curiosity would permit persuasion.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"intransigence","meaning":"Unwillingness to compromise or change a position"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":677,"targetId":677,"type":"tc","text":"The ceremonial bow expressed {{1}}, but the envoy’s refusal to concede any substantive point showed that formal {{2}} need not imply political submission.","choices":[["obeisance","contempt","indifference"],["deference","antagonism","impropriety"]],"answers":[["obeisance"],["deference"]],"explanation":"A respectful bow is obeisance and expresses deference. The sentence distinguishes ceremonial respect from surrendering policy positions.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":678,"targetId":678,"type":"tc","text":"Absorbed in his calculations, the scholar was {{1}} to the alarm; his apparent {{2}} was therefore a matter of inattention, not the {{3}} that observers first assumed.","choices":[["oblivious","attentive","susceptible"],["indifference","vigilance","agitation"],["composure","bewilderment","absorption"]],"answers":[["oblivious"],["indifference"],["composure"]],"explanation":"Failure to notice makes him oblivious. He appears indifferent, but the last contrast rejects deliberate calm, or composure, in favor of simple inattention.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"composure","meaning":"Calm control of oneself"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":679,"targetId":679,"type":"se","text":"The philosopher’s early notebooks remained {{1}} for decades, consulted only by a handful of specialists before a popular biography introduced them to a broad readership.","choices":[["obscure","little-known","celebrated","ubiquitous","fashionable","canonical"]],"answers":[["obscure","little-known"]],"explanation":"Obscure and little-known both match a work known only to specialists before wider recognition. Canonical means authoritative rather than unknown.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"little-known","meaning":"Known to relatively few people"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":680,"targetId":680,"type":"se","text":"The assistant’s {{1}} manner, full of extravagant compliments and immediate agreement with every suggestion, made even the praise-loving director uncomfortable.","choices":[["obsequious","sycophantic","defiant","equable","reserved","imperious"]],"answers":[["obsequious","sycophantic"]],"explanation":"Obsequious and sycophantic both convey excessive flattering submission. Calmness, reserve, dominance, and defiance do not explain the ingratiating excess.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"sycophantic","meaning":"Excessively flattering to gain favor"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":681,"targetId":681,"type":"tc","text":"The software is {{1}} without being defective: it still performs its original tasks perfectly, but no current system can exchange data with it.","choices":[["obsolete","innovative","versatile","ubiquitous","nascent"]],"answers":[["obsolete"]],"explanation":"A functioning tool can become obsolete when newer systems cease to support it. The contrast separates obsolescence from malfunction.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":682,"targetId":682,"type":"tc","text":"The editor called the author {{1}}, since even a demonstrable contradiction could not induce her to alter a single sentence.","choices":[["obstinate","pliant","diffident","magnanimous","tentative"]],"answers":[["obstinate"]],"explanation":"Refusal to change despite decisive evidence is obstinate. Pliancy and tentativeness would allow revision.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":683,"targetId":683,"type":"tc","text":"The discussion became {{1}} when participants began shouting over the moderator and refused repeated requests to take turns.","choices":[["obstreperous","decorous","lucid","perfunctory","soporific"]],"answers":[["obstreperous"]],"explanation":"Noisy, uncontrollable conduct is obstreperous. The participants are neither orderly nor merely mechanical or sleepy.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":684,"targetId":684,"type":"tc","text":"By making the underlying records searchable, the database could {{1}} the need for researchers to travel to the archive merely to locate a file.","choices":[["obviate","intensify","perpetuate","legitimize","underscore"]],"answers":[["obviate"]],"explanation":"Searchable records remove the need for a particular trip, so obviate fits. The alternatives maintain or emphasize that need.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":685,"targetId":685,"type":"tc","text":"The policy was judged {{1}} because it deliberately punished people for reporting abuse; the widespread {{2}} it attracted was therefore unsurprising.","choices":[["odious","beneficent","innocuous"],["censure","acclaim","deference"]],"answers":[["odious"],["censure"]],"explanation":"Retaliating against those reporting abuse makes the policy hateful, or odious. Strong public disapproval is censure, not praise or respect.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":686,"targetId":686,"type":"tc","text":"The volunteer’s help became {{1}} when she repeatedly intervened in matters outside her remit; what began as assistance was now an unwelcome {{2}}.","choices":[["officious","discreet","reticent"],["intrusion","respite","windfall"]],"answers":[["officious"],["intrusion"]],"explanation":"Unsolicited interference is officious and amounts to intrusion. Discretion and reserve would prevent the unwanted intervention.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"intrusion","meaning":"An unwelcome intervention or entry"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":687,"targetId":687,"type":"tc","text":"The unexpected silence was {{1}} to the experienced guide, who recognized it as a {{2}} of the storm that the tourists still believed would miss them.","choices":[["ominous","auspicious","jocular"],["portent","rebuttal","remedy"]],"answers":[["ominous"],["portent"]],"explanation":"The guide sees impending danger, making the silence ominous and a portent, or warning sign. A favorable sign or remedy would reverse the clue.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"portent","meaning":"A sign suggesting a future event"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":688,"targetId":688,"type":"tc","text":"References to the founder were {{1}}, appearing in every document and ceremony; her influence seemed to {{2}} the institution, leaving no corner entirely {{3}} by it.","choices":[["omnipresent","sporadic","clandestine"],["permeate","circumvent","forsake"],["untouched","shaped","pervaded"]],"answers":[["omnipresent"],["permeate"],["untouched"]],"explanation":"Presence everywhere is omnipresent. Permeate means spread throughout, so no area remains untouched rather than shaped or pervaded.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"permeate","meaning":"Spread through every part of something"},{"word":"untouched","meaning":"Not affected or altered"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":689,"targetId":689,"type":"se","text":"The reporting requirement was {{1}}, consuming three days each month that the small organization could scarcely spare from its essential work.","choices":[["onerous","burdensome","perfunctory","negligible","lucrative","effortless"]],"answers":[["onerous","burdensome"]],"explanation":"Onerous and burdensome both describe a heavy demand on scarce time. The other choices do not capture that cost.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"burdensome","meaning":"Demanding considerable effort or endurance"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":690,"targetId":690,"type":"se","text":"The regulations were {{1}} even to experienced lawyers, who could not determine which provision took precedence when two rules conflicted.","choices":[["opaque","impenetrable","pellucid","unambiguous","salutary","redundant"]],"answers":[["opaque","impenetrable"]],"explanation":"Opaque and impenetrable both mean hard to understand in this context. Clear or unambiguous rules would resolve the uncertainty.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"impenetrable","meaning":"Impossible or extremely difficult to understand"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":691,"targetId":691,"type":"tc","text":"The journalist expected praise for exposing misconduct, but a fabricated quotation instead brought {{1}} upon her and discredited the investigation.","choices":[["opprobrium","approbation","largesse","immunity","equanimity"]],"answers":[["opprobrium"]],"explanation":"Public disgrace following fabrication is opprobrium. Praise and immunity conflict with the stated discredit.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":692,"targetId":692,"type":"tc","text":"The palace’s {{1}} was not confined to its facade: even rooms visitors never entered were furnished with rare woods and elaborate goldwork.","choices":[["opulence","austerity","dilapidation","frugality","simplicity"]],"answers":[["opulence"]],"explanation":"Costly luxury throughout the building establishes opulence, not restraint or decay.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":693,"targetId":693,"type":"tc","text":"The collector disliked {{1}}: although her paintings were valuable, she displayed them privately and avoided any gesture intended to advertise her wealth.","choices":[["ostentation","reticence","privacy","restraint","erudition"]],"answers":[["ostentation"]],"explanation":"Avoiding displays meant to impress reveals dislike of ostentation. Privacy and restraint are what she favors.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":694,"targetId":694,"type":"tc","text":"Designed less for useful instruction than for attracting admiration, the presentation was {{1}}, full of gratuitous animations and conspicuous technical jargon.","choices":[["ostentatious","austere","unassuming","lucid","self-effacing"]],"answers":[["ostentatious"]],"explanation":"Showiness intended to impress is ostentatious. Restraint and clarity contradict the stated intention and gratuitous effects.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":695,"targetId":695,"type":"tc","text":"The coalition could {{1}} the leader only by winning a confidence vote; without that victory, its denunciations would remain politically {{2}}.","choices":[["oust","entrench","vindicate"],["ineffectual","decisive","binding"]],"answers":[["oust"],["ineffectual"]],"explanation":"Removing the leader is to oust him. Without the necessary vote, denunciations cannot accomplish removal and are ineffectual.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"ineffectual","meaning":"Unable to achieve the intended effect"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":696,"targetId":696,"type":"tc","text":"The hypothesis initially seemed {{1}}, but unfamiliarity alone could not {{2}} it; only a failed test would justify rejection.","choices":[["outlandish","conventional","banal"],["discredit","substantiate","corroborate"]],"answers":[["outlandish"],["discredit"]],"explanation":"An extremely unconventional proposal is outlandish. The argument says novelty is insufficient to discredit it; evidence must determine its merits.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"discredit","meaning":"Damage confidence in or respect for"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":697,"targetId":697,"type":"tc","text":"Demand began to {{1}} production, and the resulting {{2}} forced retailers to ration their remaining stock.","choices":[["outstrip","lag behind","equal"],["paucity","plethora","surfeit"]],"answers":[["outstrip"],["paucity"]],"explanation":"Demand exceeding production outstrips it and causes a shortage, or paucity. A surplus would not explain rationing.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"paucity","meaning":"An insufficient quantity"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":698,"targetId":698,"type":"tc","text":"The campaign moved from discreet lobbying to {{1}} opposition; once its objections were {{2}}, supporters could no longer claim to be {{3}} about its goals.","choices":[["overt","covert","tacit"],["public","suppressed","veiled"],["ignorant","informed","knowledgeable"]],"answers":[["overt"],["public"],["ignorant"]],"explanation":"Open opposition is overt. Public objections make professed ignorance implausible, whereas hidden objections would permit it.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"ignorant","meaning":"Lacking awareness or knowledge"},{"word":"public","meaning":"Openly available or visible to people generally"}],"section":14,"initial":"o"},{"id":699,"targetId":699,"type":"se","text":"Her reconstruction was {{1}}, built by checking every surviving invoice against independent shipping records before accepting even a minor date.","choices":[["painstaking","meticulous","cursory","desultory","haphazard","impressionistic"]],"answers":[["painstaking","meticulous"]],"explanation":"Painstaking and meticulous both describe exceptional care with detail. Haste and disorder are incompatible with checking every record.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"p"},{"id":700,"targetId":700,"type":"se","text":"The audience’s relief was {{1}}: shoulders relaxed, whispers resumed, and several people laughed as soon as the missing child appeared.","choices":[["palpable","tangible","imperceptible","feigned","hypothetical","latent"]],"answers":[["palpable","tangible"]],"explanation":"Palpable and tangible both mean readily perceptible. Multiple visible reactions contradict hidden or merely hypothetical relief.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":14,"initial":"p"},{"id":701,"targetId":701,"type":"tc","text":"The consultant sold automation as a {{1}}, promising that one system would solve problems of morale, finance, and strategy that had quite different causes.","choices":[["panacea","palliative","liability","precedent","constraint"]],"answers":[["panacea"]],"explanation":"A purported cure for every problem is a panacea. A palliative only eases symptoms, while the promise claims comprehensive resolution.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":702,"targetId":702,"type":"tc","text":"The performance lacked technical perfection, but its {{1}}—the daring entrances and assured theatrical gestures—made its imperfections easy to forgive.","choices":[["panache","diffidence","monotony","pedantry","torpor"]],"answers":[["panache"]],"explanation":"Confident stylish flair is panache. The cited theatrical assurance contrasts with timidity and dullness.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":703,"targetId":703,"type":"tc","text":"Scholars treat the dispute as {{1}} of the period’s politics because its alliances and arguments recur in nearly every other conflict they study.","choices":[["paradigmatic","anomalous","extraneous","irrelevant","idiosyncratic"]],"answers":[["paradigmatic"]],"explanation":"A case that exemplifies repeated patterns is paradigmatic. Exceptional or irrelevant cases would not provide such a model.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":704,"targetId":704,"type":"tc","text":"The policy produced a {{1}}: by making individual withdrawals easier, it increased the collective risk that nobody would be able to withdraw money at all.","choices":[["paradox","tautology","truism","digression","panegyric"]],"answers":[["paradox"]],"explanation":"Greater individual access leading to possible collective inaccessibility is an apparent contradiction, or paradox, rather than a self-evident repetition.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":705,"targetId":705,"type":"tc","text":"The result appeared {{1}} only because observers assumed that more choice always improved decisions; removing that {{2}} made the finding readily intelligible.","choices":[["paradoxical","predictable","self-evident"],["dogma","qualification","reservation"]],"answers":[["paradoxical"],["dogma"]],"explanation":"The outcome seems paradoxical under an unquestioned rigid belief, or dogma. A qualification or reservation would weaken that belief already.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"dogma","meaning":"A fixed doctrine accepted without critical examination"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":706,"targetId":706,"type":"tc","text":"The editorial’s outlook was {{1}}, treating one town’s customs as universal; the arrival of foreign correspondents supplied a welcome {{2}} to that narrow perspective.","choices":[["parochial","cosmopolitan","eclectic"],["corrective","reinforcement","ratification"]],"answers":[["parochial"],["corrective"]],"explanation":"Local narrowness is parochial. New perspectives correct rather than reinforce that assumption, so corrective supplies the contrast.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"corrective","meaning":"Something that counters an error or imbalance"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":707,"targetId":707,"type":"tc","text":"The board was {{1}} with maintenance funds, refusing even essential repairs; this apparent economy ultimately caused losses that {{2}} the initial savings.","choices":[["parsimonious","prodigal","munificent"],["outweighed","justified","minimized"]],"answers":[["parsimonious"],["outweighed"]],"explanation":"Excessive unwillingness to spend is parsimonious. Deferred repairs cost more than they saved, so losses outweighed the savings.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"outweighed","meaning":"Exceeded in importance or magnitude"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":708,"targetId":708,"type":"tc","text":"Because the reviewer was {{1}} to his former pupil, the editor assigned a second reader whose {{2}} was unquestioned; the aim was to {{3}} personal allegiance in the final verdict.","choices":[["partial","hostile","indifferent"],["impartiality","partisanship","loyalty"],["offset","enhance","reward"]],"answers":[["partial"],["impartiality"],["offset"]],"explanation":"Being partial here means favoring the pupil. An impartial second reader can offset the bias rather than amplify or reward it.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"impartiality","meaning":"Freedom from unfair preference for either side"},{"word":"offset","meaning":"Counterbalance or counteract an effect"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":709,"targetId":709,"type":"se","text":"The accounting error was {{1}}, visible in the first line of the table without calculation or specialized knowledge.","choices":[["patent","obvious","recondite","equivocal","imperceptible","latent"]],"answers":[["patent","obvious"]],"explanation":"Patent and obvious both mean readily apparent; the sentence explicitly excludes obscurity and ambiguity.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"obvious","meaning":"Easy to recognize or understand"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":710,"targetId":710,"type":"se","text":"His impatience with ceremonial speeches was regarded as a {{1}}, an irritating but minor fault beside his honesty and competence.","choices":[["peccadillo","foible","atrocity","virtue","catastrophe","achievement"]],"answers":[["peccadillo","foible"]],"explanation":"Peccadillo and foible both indicate a small fault. The context rules out major wrongdoing or a virtue.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"foible","meaning":"A minor weakness of character"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":711,"targetId":711,"type":"tc","text":"The custom is {{1}} to this valley: travelers have documented it in every village there but nowhere beyond the surrounding mountains.","choices":[["peculiar","ubiquitous","commonplace","universal","incidental"]],"answers":[["peculiar"]],"explanation":"Peculiar to means distinctive or exclusive to a place. The explicit geographic restriction excludes universality.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":712,"targetId":712,"type":"tc","text":"The reviewer behaved like a {{1}}, devoting pages to a misplaced comma while leaving the book’s central argument entirely unexamined.","choices":[["pedant","visionary","dilettante","benefactor","maverick"]],"answers":[["pedant"]],"explanation":"An excessive concern with tiny details at the expense of substance identifies a pedant, rather than a visionary or benefactor.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":713,"targetId":713,"type":"tc","text":"The instructor’s corrections became {{1}} when she insisted on an obscure convention that no longer affected either meaning or professional practice.","choices":[["pedantic","pragmatic","germane","innovative","permissive"]],"answers":[["pedantic"]],"explanation":"Insistence on pointless formal detail is pedantic. Practical relevance is explicitly denied.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":714,"targetId":714,"type":"tc","text":"Reviewers found the novel’s plot {{1}}: every apparent surprise could be predicted from a familiar formula, and no scene departed from stock conventions.","choices":[["pedestrian","innovative","idiosyncratic","protean","singular"]],"answers":[["pedestrian"]],"explanation":"An uninspired conventional plot is pedestrian. Novelty or unusual distinctiveness would contradict the repeated formula.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":715,"targetId":715,"type":"tc","text":"The essay’s language was {{1}}, but its conclusion was false; readers had confused {{2}} of expression with soundness of reasoning.","choices":[["pellucid","cryptic","opaque"],["lucidity","obscurity","prolixity"]],"answers":[["pellucid"],["lucidity"]],"explanation":"Pellucid means very clear. Lucidity likewise denotes clarity, which the sentence distinguishes from truth rather than from brevity alone.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"lucidity","meaning":"Clarity of expression or understanding"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":716,"targetId":716,"type":"tc","text":"His {{1}} for dramatic explanations made ordinary causes seem inadequate; the investigator had to resist that {{2}} and follow the unexciting evidence.","choices":[["penchant","aversion","antipathy"],["inclination","constraint","prohibition"]],"answers":[["penchant"],["inclination"]],"explanation":"A strong liking is a penchant, a form of inclination. Aversion and prohibition would deter rather than encourage dramatic explanations.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"inclination","meaning":"A tendency or preference"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":717,"targetId":717,"type":"tc","text":"After reading the farewell letter, she became {{1}}; her prolonged silence reflected {{2}}, not the indifference her companion initially supposed.","choices":[["pensive","flippant","ebullient"],["reflection","apathy","levity"]],"answers":[["pensive"],["reflection"]],"explanation":"Pensive means deeply thoughtful, often with sadness. Reflective silence differs from apathy or frivolous levity.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"reflection","meaning":"Careful sustained thought"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":718,"targetId":718,"type":"tc","text":"The dispute was {{1}}, returning under each administration; successive promises of a {{2}} settlement proved {{3}} as the same unresolved grievances resurfaced.","choices":[["perennial","unprecedented","momentary"],["lasting","temporary","provisional"],["illusory","prescient","accurate"]],"answers":[["perennial"],["lasting"],["illusory"]],"explanation":"A repeatedly returning dispute is perennial. Recurrence shows that promises of a lasting settlement were illusory, not fulfilled or accurate.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"lasting","meaning":"Continuing for a long time"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":719,"targetId":719,"type":"se","text":"The ambassador’s {{1}} became clear when confidential plans entrusted to him appeared in the rival government’s files.","choices":[["perfidy","treachery","probity","loyalty","discretion","rectitude"]],"answers":[["perfidy","treachery"]],"explanation":"Perfidy and treachery both express betrayal of trust. The transferred confidential information contradicts loyalty and integrity.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"treachery","meaning":"Betrayal of trust or loyalty"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":720,"targetId":720,"type":"se","text":"The inspection was {{1}}, completed by ticking boxes without opening the machinery or investigating the reported defect.","choices":[["perfunctory","cursory","exhaustive","painstaking","scrupulous","comprehensive"]],"answers":[["perfunctory","cursory"]],"explanation":"Perfunctory and cursory both describe a superficial inspection here. The absence of investigation excludes thoroughness.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":721,"targetId":721,"type":"tc","text":"The route was {{1}} even in fine weather, crossing unstable ledges where a single misplaced step could send a climber into the ravine.","choices":[["perilous","salubrious","innocuous","fortuitous","propitious"]],"answers":[["perilous"]],"explanation":"A serious risk of falling makes the route perilous. Favorable or harmless conditions contradict the description.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":722,"targetId":722,"type":"tc","text":"The committee treated the dispute over the meeting room as {{1}}, postponing it so that questions central to the organization’s survival could be addressed.","choices":[["peripheral","pivotal","paramount","indispensable","fundamental"]],"answers":[["peripheral"]],"explanation":"An issue set aside for central survival concerns is peripheral, or secondary, rather than essential.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":723,"targetId":723,"type":"tc","text":"The assumption was {{1}} precisely because its damage accumulated quietly: each small exclusion seemed harmless, but together they denied an entire group access.","choices":[["pernicious","salutary","innocuous","beneficent","ephemeral"]],"answers":[["pernicious"]],"explanation":"Gradually accumulating serious harm makes the assumption pernicious. Apparent harmlessness at each step conceals rather than negates that damage.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":724,"targetId":724,"type":"tc","text":"The investigation sought to identify who had helped {{1}} the fraud, not merely who had profited from it after it was complete.","choices":[["perpetrate","expose","forestall","rectify","repudiate"]],"answers":[["perpetrate"]],"explanation":"Perpetrate means commit a harmful or unlawful act. The distinction concerns participating in the fraud itself, rather than preventing or revealing it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":725,"targetId":725,"type":"tc","text":"By copying earlier catalogues without checking them, each edition helped {{1}} the original error; repetition supplied familiarity, but no additional {{2}}.","choices":[["perpetuate","eradicate","rectify"],["credence","monotony","redundancy"]],"answers":[["perpetuate"],["credence"]],"explanation":"Unchecked copying perpetuates an error. Repetition does not provide legitimate grounds for belief, or credence; monotony and redundancy would simply describe the repetition.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"credence","meaning":"Acceptance of a claim as believable"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":726,"targetId":726,"type":"tc","text":"The expedition owed its success to {{1}}, continuing after repeated equipment failures; the leader’s {{2}} effort mattered more than any single stroke of luck.","choices":[["perseverance","complacency","capitulation"],["relentless","sporadic","perfunctory"]],"answers":[["perseverance"],["relentless"]],"explanation":"Persistence despite obstacles is perseverance. Relentless effort sustains that persistence, while sporadic or mechanical effort would weaken it.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"relentless","meaning":"Continuing without yielding or stopping"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":727,"targetId":727,"type":"tc","text":"The campaigner was {{1}}, returning after every rejection, but she used no {{2}}: she stated her aims plainly and never concealed who would benefit.","choices":[["pertinacious","irresolute","apathetic"],["guile","candor","transparency"]],"answers":[["pertinacious"],["guile"]],"explanation":"Stubborn persistence is pertinacious. Her open disclosure excludes guile, or deceptive cleverness, rather than candor.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"guile","meaning":"Clever deception used to gain an advantage"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":728,"targetId":728,"type":"tc","text":"The anecdote was {{1}} only if the two cases shared the same mechanism; without that {{2}}, its resemblance to the present dispute was {{3}} rather than explanatory.","choices":[["pertinent","extraneous","irrelevant"],["qualification","certainty","endorsement"],["superficial","profound","causal"]],"answers":[["pertinent"],["qualification"],["superficial"]],"explanation":"Pertinent means relevant. The conditional limitation is a qualification, and absent a shared mechanism the similarity is merely superficial.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"qualification","meaning":"A reservation that limits the force of a statement"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":729,"targetId":729,"type":"se","text":"The influence of the reform was {{1}}, detectable in the language of classrooms, offices, and courts rather than confined to any one institution.","choices":[["pervasive","widespread","localized","negligible","sporadic","clandestine"]],"answers":[["pervasive","widespread"]],"explanation":"Pervasive and widespread both convey broad reach. The numerous settings rule out localization or scant presence.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"widespread","meaning":"Occurring across a broad area or range"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":730,"targetId":730,"type":"se","text":"Her {{1}} response to a minor delay—pouting and refusing to speak to anyone—struck colleagues as more appropriate to a disappointed child than to a senior official.","choices":[["petulant","peevish","magnanimous","equable","phlegmatic","conciliatory"]],"answers":[["petulant","peevish"]],"explanation":"Petulant and peevish describe childish irritability; calmness and generosity would not fit the sulking.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"peevish","meaning":"Irritable in a petty or childish way"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":731,"targetId":731,"type":"tc","text":"Although the program brought the founder little recognition, it remained {{1}} in purpose, directing its entire budget toward improving strangers’ welfare.","choices":[["philanthropic","mercenary","vindictive","self-serving","avaricious"]],"answers":[["philanthropic"]],"explanation":"An aim of helping others rather than gaining money or recognition makes the program philanthropic.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":732,"targetId":732,"type":"tc","text":"The novelist called the developer a {{1}} for judging the old theater solely by its resale value and dismissing all discussion of its artistic significance.","choices":[["philistine","connoisseur","aesthete","virtuoso","polymath"]],"answers":[["philistine"]],"explanation":"Indifference to artistic value identifies a philistine. Artistic appreciation or broad learning would not explain the dismissal.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":733,"targetId":733,"type":"tc","text":"While the other traders panicked, the {{1}} analyst calmly reviewed her figures and declined to let the commotion alter her judgment.","choices":[["phlegmatic","mercurial","irascible","histrionic","impetuous"]],"answers":[["phlegmatic"]],"explanation":"A calm, unexcitable temperament is phlegmatic. Emotional volatility and rashness contradict her response.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":734,"targetId":734,"type":"tc","text":"The diaries reveal a genuinely {{1}} person: prayer organized her days even when no audience was present to reward a display of devotion.","choices":[["pious","impious","secular","profane","cynical"]],"answers":[["pious"]],"explanation":"Private sustained religious devotion supports pious in its sincere sense, excluding mere performance and irreligion.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":735,"targetId":735,"type":"tc","text":"The verdict was {{1}}, expressing a complicated distinction in one sharp sentence; its {{2}} style concealed neither ambiguity nor an absence of thought.","choices":[["pithy","prolix","rambling"],["unadorned","florid","baroque"]],"answers":[["pithy"],["unadorned"]],"explanation":"Brief but meaningful language is pithy. Plain, unadorned expression complements its economy, unlike elaborate decoration.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"unadorned","meaning":"Plain and without decorative embellishment"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":736,"targetId":736,"type":"tc","text":"The company hoped a refund would {{1}} its customers, but acknowledgment without repayment had already made them skeptical of merely {{2}} concessions.","choices":[["placate","antagonize","incite"],["nominal","substantial","costly"]],"answers":[["placate"],["nominal"]],"explanation":"A real refund aims to calm anger, or placate. Earlier empty acknowledgment makes customers distrust concessions in name only, or nominal ones.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":737,"targetId":737,"type":"tc","text":"The lake appeared {{1}}, but the still surface gave a misleading impression of conditions below; unseen currents made swimmers’ confidence {{2}}.","choices":[["placid","turbulent","tempestuous"],["unwarranted","judicious","prescient"]],"answers":[["placid"],["unwarranted"]],"explanation":"Placid describes the calm appearance, not actual safety. Hidden currents make confidence unjustified, or unwarranted.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"unwarranted","meaning":"Not supported by sufficient reason"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":738,"targetId":738,"type":"tc","text":"The singer’s {{1}} tone made even the neutral words sound like a lament; the effect came from emotional {{2}}, not verbal {{3}}.","choices":[["plaintive","jubilant","triumphal"],["expression","detachment","restraint"],["meaning","ornament","brevity"]],"answers":[["plaintive"],["expression"],["meaning"]],"explanation":"A sorrowful tone is plaintive. The neutral words establish that emotional expression supplies the lament rather than their literal meaning.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"expression","meaning":"Communication of a feeling or idea"},{"word":"meaning","meaning":"The sense conveyed by words or signs"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":739,"targetId":739,"type":"se","text":"The apprentice’s habits were still {{1}}, readily reshaped by instruction before years of repetition could make them rigid.","choices":[["plastic","moldable","immutable","entrenched","inveterate","refractory"]],"answers":[["plastic","moldable"]],"explanation":"Plastic and moldable both mean capable of being shaped, here by teaching. The alternatives emphasize resistance or established habit.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"moldable","meaning":"Capable of being shaped"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":740,"targetId":740,"type":"se","text":"The address offered a {{1}} about following one’s dreams, a familiar reassurance repeated so often that it communicated little to the graduates.","choices":[["platitude","cliche","revelation","paradox","innovation","discovery"]],"answers":[["platitude","cliche"]],"explanation":"Platitude and cliche both identify an overused, uninformative expression. Novel insight would contradict familiarity and repetition.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"cliche","meaning":"An overused expression or idea"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":741,"targetId":741,"type":"tc","text":"The adviser proved remarkably {{1}}, abandoning his carefully stated objection as soon as the chair signaled that agreement would be appreciated.","choices":[["pliant","intransigent","obdurate","steadfast","recalcitrant"]],"answers":[["pliant"]],"explanation":"Readiness to yield to pressure makes him pliant. Each alternative would predict resistance rather than swift compliance.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":742,"targetId":742,"type":"tc","text":"The biography’s narrative is {{1}}: it advances date by date with painstaking transitions, rarely achieving the momentum suggested by its dramatic subject.","choices":[["plodding","brisk","mercurial","effervescent","precipitous"]],"answers":[["plodding"]],"explanation":"Slow, laborious progress is plodding. A dramatic subject does not make the narration rapid or lively.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":743,"targetId":743,"type":"tc","text":"The apparently spontaneous withdrawal was a {{1}}, designed to induce the rival to overextend rather than to end the contest.","choices":[["ploy","capitulation","blunder","respite","penance"]],"answers":[["ploy"]],"explanation":"A calculated tactical maneuver is a ploy. The explicit purpose rules out surrender or accidental error.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":744,"targetId":744,"type":"tc","text":"Though inexperienced and badly outnumbered, the {{1}} volunteers refused to abandon the rescue attempt when the first route failed.","choices":[["plucky","craven","timorous","pusillanimous","apathetic"]],"answers":[["plucky"]],"explanation":"Bravery and determination despite danger make them plucky. Fearfulness and indifference contradict their persistence.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":745,"targetId":745,"type":"tc","text":"The letter was {{1}} because its cheerful plans would never be fulfilled; knowing the writer’s fate gave the simple details an emotional {{2}}.","choices":[["poignant","trite","insipid"],["depth","detachment","levity"]],"answers":[["poignant"],["depth"]],"explanation":"Unfulfilled hopes make the letter painfully moving, or poignant. Knowledge of the fate adds emotional depth rather than distance or lightness.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"depth","meaning":"Substantial intellectual or emotional significance"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":746,"targetId":746,"type":"tc","text":"Her {{1}} during the hostile interview reflected practice rather than lack of feeling; visible anger would have undermined the {{2}} judgment she wanted to convey.","choices":[["poise","agitation","fluster"],["judicious","impetuous","vindictive"]],"answers":[["poise"],["judicious"]],"explanation":"Poise is controlled composure. She wants to project careful, judicious judgment rather than impulsiveness or revenge.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":747,"targetId":747,"type":"tc","text":"The issue began to {{1}} the association, drawing members into opposed camps; attempts to {{2}} between those camps only made the chair seem indecisive.","choices":[["polarize","unify","reconcile"],["oscillate","adjudicate","mediate"]],"answers":[["polarize"],["oscillate"]],"explanation":"Division into opposed groups is to polarize. Repeatedly swinging between positions is to oscillate and supports the charge of indecision; mediation need not.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"oscillate","meaning":"Move repeatedly between opposing positions"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":748,"targetId":748,"type":"tc","text":"The essay was openly {{1}}, attacking the prevailing account rather than neutrally summarizing it; its author’s {{2}} stance was deliberate, but this did not excuse {{3}} quotations.","choices":[["polemical","conciliatory","dispassionate"],["partisan","impartial","equivocal"],["inaccurate","verbatim","relevant"]],"answers":[["polemical"],["partisan"],["inaccurate"]],"explanation":"Argumentative attack makes the essay polemical and explicitly committed to a side, or partisan. A declared position cannot justify inaccurate evidence.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"inaccurate","meaning":"Not conforming to fact or correct detail"},{"word":"partisan","meaning":"Strongly favoring a particular side"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":749,"targetId":749,"type":"se","text":"Equally accomplished in mathematics, music, and historical scholarship, she was a {{1}} whose reputation could not be contained within a single discipline.","choices":[["polymath","universal scholar","specialist","dilettante","pedant","neophyte"]],"answers":[["polymath","universal scholar"]],"explanation":"Polymath and universal scholar both describe substantial learning across disciplines. Amateur interest or narrow specialization would not match equal accomplishment.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"universal scholar","meaning":"A person with extensive learning in many disciplines"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":750,"targetId":750,"type":"se","text":"The preface’s {{1}} was evident in the author’s insistence that his modest handbook would inaugurate a new era in human thought.","choices":[["pomposity","self-importance","humility","diffidence","modesty","circumspection"]],"answers":[["pomposity","self-importance"]],"explanation":"Pomposity and self-importance both fit the inflated claim for a modest book. Humility and caution would restrain it.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"self-importance","meaning":"An exaggerated estimate of one’s own significance"}],"section":15,"initial":"p"},{"id":751,"targetId":751,"type":"tc","text":"The early resignations seemed to {{1}} a wider collapse, and subsequent departures confirmed that they had been warning signs rather than isolated incidents.","choices":[["portend","avert","conceal","forestall","rectify"]],"answers":[["portend"]],"explanation":"Warning signs indicate what is coming, or portend it. They do not prevent or repair the later collapse.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":752,"targetId":752,"type":"tc","text":"The mayor adopted a {{1}} approach, keeping the imperfect arrangement because it delivered services reliably while her theoretically elegant alternatives remained unworkable.","choices":[["pragmatic","doctrinaire","utopian","dogmatic","quixotic"]],"answers":[["pragmatic"]],"explanation":"Choosing what functions in practice is pragmatic. Rigid ideology or impractical idealism would favor the unusable alternatives.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":753,"targetId":753,"type":"tc","text":"The institution’s finances were {{1}}: losing even one small grant would leave it unable to meet salaries for the next month.","choices":[["precarious","robust","invulnerable","secure","ample"]],"answers":[["precarious"]],"explanation":"Dependence on a small grant for basic obligations makes its position precarious, or dangerously unstable.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":754,"targetId":754,"type":"tc","text":"The announcement could {{1}} a crisis by prompting withdrawals before the bank had arranged replacement funds.","choices":[["precipitate","forestall","resolve","mitigate","avert"]],"answers":[["precipitate"]],"explanation":"Causing a crisis suddenly is to precipitate it. The sentence describes a trigger, not prevention or relief.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":755,"targetId":755,"type":"tc","text":"The currency’s {{1}} fall left traders no time to revise their positions; a change expected to take months happened in minutes, making earlier forecasts {{2}}.","choices":[["precipitous","gradual","imperceptible"],["erroneous","prescient","accurate"]],"answers":[["precipitous"],["erroneous"]],"explanation":"A dangerously sudden decline is precipitous. Its speed contradicts the forecasts, making them erroneous.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"erroneous","meaning":"Incorrect or mistaken"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":756,"targetId":756,"type":"tc","text":"The agreement did not {{1}} later cooperation; it merely required that any {{2}} partnership receive separate approval.","choices":[["preclude","permit","facilitate"],["prospective","retrospective","defunct"]],"answers":[["preclude"],["prospective"]],"explanation":"The agreement does not prevent future cooperation, hence preclude. Prospective means contemplated for the future, unlike retrospective or already ended.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"prospective","meaning":"Expected or considered as a future possibility"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":757,"targetId":757,"type":"tc","text":"The child’s {{1}} command of algebra astonished teachers, but early skill was not {{2}} evidence that she would enjoy mathematics throughout adulthood.","choices":[["precocious","belated","rudimentary"],["sufficient","inconclusive","equivocal"]],"answers":[["precocious"],["sufficient"]],"explanation":"Unusually early ability is precocious. The contrast says early skill is not enough, or sufficient, to establish lifelong enjoyment; the alternatives reverse that inference.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"sufficient","meaning":"Enough for the purpose at hand"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":758,"targetId":758,"type":"tc","text":"The editor faced a {{1}}: publishing immediately risked error, but delay would allow competitors to {{2}}; neither choice was free of a serious {{3}}.","choices":[["predicament","windfall","respite"],["prevail","flounder","capitulate"],["liability","advantage","boon"]],"answers":[["predicament"],["prevail"],["liability"]],"explanation":"The conflicting risks create a predicament. Delay helps competitors succeed, or prevail, while both alternatives carry a disadvantage, or liability.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"prevail","meaning":"Succeed despite resistance or obstacles"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":759,"targetId":759,"type":"se","text":"Her {{1}} for spare prose was evident in every revision: she consistently chose a short, plain phrase over an equally accurate ornate one.","choices":[["predilection","preference","aversion","antipathy","indifference","hostility"]],"answers":[["predilection","preference"]],"explanation":"Predilection and preference both describe a liking manifested through repeated choice. Aversion and indifference contradict the pattern.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"preference","meaning":"A greater liking for one option than another"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":760,"targetId":760,"type":"se","text":"The conspirators had time to {{1}} the theft, discussing its timing and assigning roles for weeks before entering the building.","choices":[["premeditate","plan","improvise","abort","disavow","expose"]],"answers":[["premeditate","plan"]],"explanation":"Premeditate and plan both mean arrange in advance. Weeks of preparation rule out improvisation or abandonment.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"plan","meaning":"Decide in advance how to act"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":761,"targetId":761,"type":"tc","text":"Her warning proved {{1}} when the industry encountered precisely the shortage she had predicted years before anyone else took it seriously.","choices":[["prescient","myopic","belated","erroneous","gratuitous"]],"answers":[["prescient"]],"explanation":"Accurate anticipation of a future problem is prescient. Myopia and error would not fit the confirmed prediction.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":762,"targetId":762,"type":"tc","text":"It was {{1}} of the new assistant to announce a change in policy before asking the director, since no authority to make such decisions had been delegated.","choices":[["presumptuous","deferential","diffident","circumspect","obsequious"]],"answers":[["presumptuous"]],"explanation":"Overstepping one’s authority with unwarranted confidence is presumptuous. Submission and caution would entail consultation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":763,"targetId":763,"type":"tc","text":"The critic found the installation {{1}} because its grand claims to philosophical depth far exceeded the slight ideas it actually expressed.","choices":[["pretentious","unassuming","modest","self-effacing","unpretending"]],"answers":[["pretentious"]],"explanation":"Claiming significance beyond real merit is pretentious. The mismatch rules out modest self-presentation.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":764,"targetId":764,"type":"tc","text":"Asked whether she had approved the payment, the official chose to {{1}}, discussing the complexity of departmental procedures without answering yes or no.","choices":[["prevaricate","confess","clarify","avow","corroborate"]],"answers":[["prevaricate"]],"explanation":"Evasive speech that avoids a direct answer is to prevaricate. The alternatives require greater directness or verification.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":765,"targetId":765,"type":"tc","text":"For the committee, safety was the {{1}} consideration; cost mattered only after a proposal met a {{2}} threshold of protection.","choices":[["prime","peripheral","incidental"],["sufficient","negligible","illusory"]],"answers":[["prime"],["sufficient"]],"explanation":"Prime means most important. A sufficient threshold is enough to meet the protective requirement, unlike negligible or imaginary protection.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"sufficient","meaning":"Enough for the purpose at hand"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":766,"targetId":766,"type":"tc","text":"The island remained {{1}} because visitors were restricted to one harbor; the interior’s {{2}} condition contrasted with the development visible along the neighboring coast.","choices":[["pristine","despoiled","degraded"],["unsullied","depleted","ravaged"]],"answers":[["pristine"],["unsullied"]],"explanation":"An unspoiled island is pristine and unsullied. Restricting access explains its contrast with development elsewhere.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"unsullied","meaning":"Not spoiled or contaminated"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":767,"targetId":767,"type":"tc","text":"The treasurer’s {{1}} was demonstrated by her refusal of a secret commission; she treated the offer as an affront to {{2}}, not an opportunity.","choices":[["probity","venality","duplicity"],["integrity","avarice","opportunism"]],"answers":[["probity"],["integrity"]],"explanation":"Refusing corrupt gain establishes probity and integrity. Greed and opportunism would make the offer attractive rather than offensive.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"integrity","meaning":"Consistent honesty and adherence to moral principles"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":768,"targetId":768,"type":"tc","text":"A {{1}} for treating exceptions as typical distorted his conclusions; without a broader sample, the resulting {{2}} were {{3}} rather than reliable accounts of the population.","choices":[["proclivity","aversion","inability"],["generalizations","reservations","qualifications"],["biased","representative","disinterested"]],"answers":[["proclivity"],["generalizations"],["biased"]],"explanation":"A habitual inclination is a proclivity. General claims drawn from atypical cases are generalizations that become biased, not representative.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"biased","meaning":"Unfairly favoring a particular side"},{"word":"generalizations","meaning":"Broad conclusions applied beyond particular observations"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":769,"targetId":769,"type":"se","text":"The heir was {{1}}, exhausting a substantial inheritance on lavish entertainments in less than a year.","choices":[["prodigal","extravagant","frugal","parsimonious","abstemious","miserly"]],"answers":[["prodigal","extravagant"]],"explanation":"Prodigal and extravagant both describe excessive expenditure. Each distractor implies restraint or unwillingness to spend.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":770,"targetId":770,"type":"se","text":"The project required a {{1}} quantity of stone, enough to keep several quarries operating at full capacity for a decade.","choices":[["prodigious","immense","paltry","negligible","nominal","scant"]],"answers":[["prodigious","immense"]],"explanation":"Prodigious and immense both mean extraordinarily large. The sustained demand on several quarries excludes small quantities.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"immense","meaning":"Extremely large"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":771,"targetId":771,"type":"tc","text":"She was {{1}} in the language, handling ordinary correspondence accurately and independently, although she lacked the stylistic range of a literary translator.","choices":[["proficient","inept","untutored","inarticulate","incompetent"]],"answers":[["proficient"]],"explanation":"Accurate independent performance establishes proficiency, even without exceptional literary skill. The qualification limits mastery rather than denying competence.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":772,"targetId":772,"type":"tc","text":"The administration’s spending was {{1}}, dissipating reserves on avoidable luxuries even as essential repairs went unfunded.","choices":[["profligate","parsimonious","frugal","provident","austere"]],"answers":[["profligate"]],"explanation":"Reckless waste amid neglected necessities is profligate, the opposite of restraint or foresight.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":773,"targetId":773,"type":"tc","text":"The article’s conclusion was {{1}}, forcing specialists to rethink assumptions that had organized the field for decades rather than merely adjusting one estimate.","choices":[["profound","trivial","superficial","perfunctory","pedestrian"]],"answers":[["profound"]],"explanation":"A conclusion changing foundational assumptions is profound. The explicit contrast rules out superficial or ordinary importance.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":774,"targetId":774,"type":"tc","text":"Readers mistook the essay’s obscurity for {{1}}, assuming that an argument difficult to understand must also be intellectually deep.","choices":[["profundity","lucidity","brevity","superficiality","precision"]],"answers":[["profundity"]],"explanation":"The mistaken inference equates difficulty with profundity, or depth. Clarity and brevity are not what readers inferred.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":775,"targetId":775,"type":"tc","text":"The apology was {{1}}, filling pages with expressions of regret, yet its author’s refusal to repair the damage made that apparent {{2}} hard to trust.","choices":[["profuse","terse","laconic"],["contrition","defiance","indifference"]],"answers":[["profuse"],["contrition"]],"explanation":"Abundant expression makes the apology profuse. It appears to signal remorse, or contrition, though lack of corrective action undermines that impression.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":776,"targetId":776,"type":"tc","text":"Once licensing restrictions disappeared, small publishers began to {{1}}; their multiplication changed the industry’s {{2}} to distribute unconventional work.","choices":[["proliferate","dwindle","consolidate"],["capacity","reluctance","inability"]],"answers":[["proliferate"],["capacity"]],"explanation":"Rapid multiplication is to proliferate. More publishers increase the capacity, or ability, to distribute varied work rather than merely its reluctance.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"capacity","meaning":"Ability to perform a function or contain something"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":777,"targetId":777,"type":"tc","text":"The report was {{1}}, taking thirty pages to state what a careful summary conveyed in two; the editor urged {{2}} rather than additional examples.","choices":[["prolix","succinct","laconic"],["concision","elaboration","digression"]],"answers":[["prolix"],["concision"]],"explanation":"Excessively long-winded writing is prolix. The proposed remedy is concision, not more elaboration or detours.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"concision","meaning":"Economy of expression without unnecessary words"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":778,"targetId":778,"type":"tc","text":"The network could {{1}} rumors faster than corrections; its speed therefore increased the {{2}} of false claims without improving their {{3}}.","choices":[["propagate","suppress","quash"],["reach","isolation","obscurity"],["veracity","notoriety","visibility"]],"answers":[["propagate"],["reach"],["veracity"]],"explanation":"To spread rumors is to propagate them. Faster spread increases reach, while truthfulness, or veracity, remains unchanged by speed.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"reach","meaning":"The extent of an audience or influence"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":779,"targetId":779,"type":"se","text":"The model revealed a {{1}} to overestimate rare outcomes, a recurring bias that persisted across different samples.","choices":[["propensity","tendency","aversion","inability","prohibition","constraint"]],"answers":[["propensity","tendency"]],"explanation":"Propensity and tendency both identify a recurring inclination. The observation is neither avoidance nor a rule restricting behavior.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"tendency","meaning":"A recurring inclination toward an action"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":780,"targetId":780,"type":"se","text":"The novel seemed {{1}} when technologies imagined in its pages became commonplace decades after publication.","choices":[["prophetic","predictive","retrospective","anachronistic","derivative","nostalgic"]],"answers":[["prophetic","predictive"]],"explanation":"Prophetic and predictive both describe anticipation of later events. Looking backward does not explain the subsequent realization of imagined technologies.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"predictive","meaning":"Indicating what will occur in the future"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":781,"targetId":781,"type":"tc","text":"Conditions for the merger were {{1}}: financing was available, regulators were receptive, and both workforces supported the proposed terms.","choices":[["propitious","inimical","adverse","inauspicious","prohibitive"]],"answers":[["propitious"]],"explanation":"Multiple favorable conditions make the circumstances propitious. The alternatives imply obstacles rather than support.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":782,"targetId":782,"type":"tc","text":"The ambassador’s concern for {{1}} extended to seating arrangements, forms of address, and every other convention governing formal diplomatic behavior.","choices":[["propriety","expediency","profitability","novelty","improvisation"]],"answers":[["propriety"]],"explanation":"Observance of appropriate conduct and convention is propriety. Profit and novelty do not govern the listed etiquette.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":783,"targetId":783,"type":"tc","text":"Behind the legend of a sudden inspiration lay a {{1}} reality: years of routine measurements, revisions, and equipment maintenance.","choices":[["prosaic","numinous","romantic","fantastical","transcendent"]],"answers":[["prosaic"]],"explanation":"Ordinary routine contrasts with a romantic legend, making prosaic appropriate. Mystical or extraordinary choices would preserve the legend.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":784,"targetId":784,"type":"tc","text":"The arrival of the missing map seemed {{1}}, occurring just when the expedition had exhausted every other means of finding a safe route.","choices":[["providential","inauspicious","deleterious","ominous","calamitous"]],"answers":[["providential"]],"explanation":"An exceptionally fortunate, timely arrival seems providential, as though arranged for the travelers’ benefit. Harmful or ominous alternatives reverse the outcome.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":785,"targetId":785,"type":"tc","text":"The critic’s perspective was {{1}}, judging unfamiliar traditions solely by the standards of her own town; greater cultural {{2}} might have broadened her assessment.","choices":[["provincial","cosmopolitan","ecumenical"],["exposure","isolation","confinement"]],"answers":[["provincial"],["exposure"]],"explanation":"Narrow local judgment is provincial. Exposure to other cultures could broaden it, whereas isolation would reinforce it.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"exposure","meaning":"Experience of or contact with something"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":786,"targetId":786,"type":"tc","text":"The article was deliberately {{1}}, phrasing a modest disagreement as an attack on the field’s integrity; the editor predicted an {{2}} response.","choices":[["provocative","conciliatory","anodyne"],["indignant","apathetic","unruffled"]],"answers":[["provocative"],["indignant"]],"explanation":"An attack designed to provoke reaction is provocative. Indignation fits an insult to integrity, unlike indifference or calm.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"indignant","meaning":"Angry at perceived insult or unfairness"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":787,"targetId":787,"type":"tc","text":"Her athletic {{1}} was unquestioned, but the team appointed her captain for judgment rather than physical skill; leadership demanded more than technical {{2}}.","choices":[["prowess","ineptitude","frailty"],["expertise","incompetence","uncertainty"]],"answers":[["prowess"],["expertise"]],"explanation":"Exceptional athletic skill is prowess. Expertise names technical skill, which the sentence says is necessary but insufficient for leadership.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"expertise","meaning":"Specialized knowledge or skill"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":788,"targetId":788,"type":"tc","text":"Unable to attend, the shareholder appointed a {{1}} to vote on her behalf; this {{2}} had authority to convey her decision but no {{3}} to substitute his own preferences.","choices":[["proxy","spectator","adversary"],["representative","rival","bystander"],["discretion","obligation","compulsion"]],"answers":[["proxy"],["representative"],["discretion"]],"explanation":"An authorized substitute is a proxy and representative. He lacks discretion, or freedom of judgment, to change her vote; obligation would imply he must change it.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"discretion","meaning":"Freedom to exercise independent judgment"},{"word":"representative","meaning":"Someone authorized to act for another"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":789,"targetId":789,"type":"se","text":"The committee’s response was {{1}}, delaying an irreversible commitment until the uncertain estimates could be checked.","choices":[["prudent","judicious","rash","precipitate","impetuous","reckless"]],"answers":[["prudent","judicious"]],"explanation":"Prudent and judicious both express sensible caution in the face of uncertainty. Every distractor implies haste or disregard of risk.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":790,"targetId":790,"type":"se","text":"The biography’s {{1}} attention to intimate scandals eclipsed its subject’s public achievements, dwelling on sexual details with little historical relevance.","choices":[["prurient","salacious","dispassionate","austere","reticent","decorous"]],"answers":[["prurient","salacious"]],"explanation":"Prurient and salacious both describe an excessive interest in sexual material. Restraint or dispassion would not produce the intrusive focus.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"salacious","meaning":"Showing excessive interest in sexual detail"}],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":791,"targetId":791,"type":"tc","text":"The debate deteriorated into {{1}} taunts, with participants mocking one another’s nicknames rather than addressing the policy under discussion.","choices":[["puerile","sagacious","erudite","judicious","cogent"]],"answers":[["puerile"]],"explanation":"Childishly silly mockery is puerile. Learned or reasoned arguments would address the policy instead.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":792,"targetId":792,"type":"tc","text":"The interviewer was so {{1}} that even an agreement became an occasion for confrontation; she seemed less interested in clarification than in finding a fight.","choices":[["pugnacious","conciliatory","placid","retiring","deferential"]],"answers":[["pugnacious"]],"explanation":"Readiness to quarrel makes her pugnacious. Calmness or conciliation would not turn agreement into conflict.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":793,"targetId":793,"type":"tc","text":"He was {{1}} about protocol, checking the exact rank of every guest before determining the order of introductions.","choices":[["punctilious","cavalier","slapdash","negligent","improvisational"]],"answers":[["punctilious"]],"explanation":"Exact attention to etiquette is punctilious. Carelessness and improvisation contradict checking every rank.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"p"},{"id":794,"targetId":794,"type":"tc","text":"The payment was technically permitted, but the researcher still felt a {{1}}, wondering whether accepting it would compromise the independence of her conclusions.","choices":[["qualm","conviction","certainty","euphoria","resolution"]],"answers":[["qualm"]],"explanation":"A troubling doubt is a qualm. Confidence or happiness would not describe her concern about compromised independence.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"q"},{"id":795,"targetId":795,"type":"tc","text":"The discovery placed the curator in a {{1}}: disclosure could jeopardize the exhibition, while silence would violate her {{2}} to the public.","choices":[["quandary","respite","windfall"],["obligation","aversion","indifference"]],"answers":[["quandary"],["obligation"]],"explanation":"Two costly alternatives create a quandary. A duty to inform is an obligation, making concealment ethically problematic.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"obligation","meaning":"A duty or binding responsibility"}],"section":16,"initial":"q"},{"id":796,"targetId":796,"type":"tc","text":"Officials imposed a {{1}} on the affected shipment to keep the suspected contamination from spreading; the temporary {{2}} was precautionary, not punitive.","choices":[["quarantine","subsidy","waiver"],["isolation","dispersal","distribution"]],"answers":[["quarantine"],["isolation"]],"explanation":"Quarantine separates a possible source of danger; isolation describes that separation. Distributing it would frustrate the preventive aim.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"isolation","meaning":"Separation from others"}],"section":16,"initial":"q"},{"id":797,"targetId":797,"type":"tc","text":"The judge could {{1}} the earlier order once its legal basis had been disproved; leaving it in force would {{2}} a restriction that the law did not authorize.","choices":[["quash","ratify","endorse"],["perpetuate","terminate","obviate"]],"answers":[["quash"],["perpetuate"]],"explanation":"To annul the invalid order is to quash it. Leaving it operative would perpetuate, rather than end, the unauthorized restriction.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[],"section":16,"initial":"q"},{"id":798,"targetId":798,"type":"tc","text":"The witness tried to {{1}} over an insignificant date, but the investigator refused to let that {{2}} obscure the {{3}} issue of whether the funds had been diverted.","choices":[["quibble","concur","acquiesce"],["triviality","principle","revelation"],["substantive","incidental","peripheral"]],"answers":[["quibble"],["triviality"],["substantive"]],"explanation":"Petty objection is to quibble. The minor date is a triviality, contrasted with the substantive question of diverted funds.","difficulty":"hard","extras":[{"word":"substantive","meaning":"Concerning the essential or significant matter"},{"word":"triviality","meaning":"A matter of little importance"}],"section":16,"initial":"q"},{"id":799,"targetId":799,"type":"se","text":"The fault remained {{1}} for decades, showing no detectable movement before instruments registered the first renewed activity.","choices":[["quiescent","dormant","turbulent","volatile","kinetic","incessant"]],"answers":[["quiescent","dormant"]],"explanation":"Quiescent and dormant both mean inactive or resting. The long absence of movement excludes continual activity.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"dormant","meaning":"Temporarily inactive"}],"section":16,"initial":"q"},{"id":800,"targetId":800,"type":"se","text":"The café’s {{1}} decor—mismatched clocks, handmade signs, and chairs of improbable shapes—gave it an odd but appealing individuality.","choices":[["quirky","offbeat","conventional","uniform","standardized","utilitarian"]],"answers":[["quirky","offbeat"]],"explanation":"Quirky and offbeat both capture appealing unconventionality. Standardization and convention would erase the described individuality.","difficulty":"medium","extras":[{"word":"offbeat","meaning":"Unusual in an interesting or appealing way"}],"section":16,"initial":"q"}]);
